n\n---\n\n#### 2.  The three sunny lines  \n\nThe three lines determined by the sunny pairs are:\n\n1. **Line \\(L_{1}\\)** through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\; y = x .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Line \\(L_{2}\\)** through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   L_{2}:\\; y-2 = -\\frac12\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Line \\(L_{3}\\)** through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   L_{3}:\\; y-3 = -2\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   y = -2x + 5 .\n   \\]\n\nEach of the slopes \\(1,\\,-\\dfrac12,\\,-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently all three lines are sunny.  \n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Verification that every required point lies on a sunny line  \n\n| point \\((a,b)\\) | line containing it | check |\n|-----------------|--------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n\nAll six points of \\(T_{3}\\) belong to at least one of the three lines, and the three lines are distinct and sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together cover every point required for \\(n=3\\). Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the value }k=3\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]"
