            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\) (horizontal), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and not \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nWe must find, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), whether there exist three distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{3}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\) (all three lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: x=1,\\qquad L_{2}: y=1,\\qquad L_{3}: x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{1}\\) is vertical, \\(L_{2}\\) horizontal, \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence none is sunny.  \nChecking each point of \\(T_{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{1}\\\\\n(1,3) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{2}\\\\\n(2,2) & L_{3}\\;(2+2=4)\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2},L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll six points are covered, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: x=1,\\qquad L_{2}: y=1,\\qquad L_{3}: y=x .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny; \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are non‑sunny.  \nCoverage:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{1}\\\\\n(1,3) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{2}\\\\\n(2,2) & L_{3}\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k=1\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=2\\) (two sunny lines, one non‑sunny line) – impossibility\n\nAssume three distinct lines \\(L_{a},L_{b},L_{c}\\) cover \\(T_{3}\\) with exactly two sunny lines.  \nLet \\(L\\) be the unique non‑sunny line; it must be of one of the three types:\n\n* **Vertical** \\(x=c\\).  \n  The only possible vertical lines intersecting \\(T_{3}\\) are \\(x=1,2,3\\).\n\n  *If \\(L:x=1\\)*, it covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Any line through any two of these three points is either vertical (\\(x=2\\)), horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), or slope \\(-1\\) (\\((2,2)-(3,1)\\)).  \n  Hence every line containing two of the remaining points is non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non‑sunny.\n\n  *If \\(L:x=2\\)*, it covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}\\) still contains three points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate; any line through two of them would be vertical, i.e. non‑sunny.  \n  Thus at least two non‑sunny lines would be needed – contradiction.\n\n  *If \\(L:x=3\\)*, it covers only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The three points with \\(x=1\\) again force at least two non‑sunny lines.  \n\n  Hence a vertical non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n* **Horizontal** \\(y=c\\).  \n  By symmetry the same argument (using the three points with \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\) etc.) shows impossibility.\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\)**, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  Possible values are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n  *If \\(c=4\\)*, \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The remaining points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) are pairwise either vertical, horizontal