            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma (Sunny covering of a triangular lattice)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe argue by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and contains the point.\n\n*Inductive step.* Assume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); let \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\) be distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).  \nConsider \\(T_{m+1}=T_m\\cup H_{m+1}\\) where  \n\n\\[\nH_{m+1}=\\{(a,m+2-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis the new hypotenuse.  \nChoose a rational number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope}(\\ell_i)\\;(1\\le i\\le m).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{m+1}:\\; y-\\bigl(1\\bigr)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{equivalently }y=\\alpha x+1-\\alpha(m+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) passes through the endpoint \\((m+1,1)\\in H_{m+1}\\); because its slope is not \\(-1\\), it meets the hypotenuse \\(H_{m+1}\\) in exactly this point.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) we have \\(b-a\\neq 1-\\alpha(m+1)\\) (otherwise the line through \\((a,b)\\) with slope \\(\\alpha\\) would have slope \\(-1\\)), hence \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) does not contain any point of \\(T_m\\).  \nThus the \\(m+1\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{m+1}\\) are distinct, all are sunny, and together they cover \\(T_{m+1}\\). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary pair \\((n,k)\\)\n\nFix integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\nu_i:\\;x=i,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are pairwise distinct and are not sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   By the Lemma there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) covering the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write each \\(\\sigma_j\\) in slope–intercept form \\(\\sigma_j:\\;y=\\alpha_j x+\\beta_j\\) (\\(\\alpha_j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Translate every \\(\\sigma_j\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the translated line is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda_j:\\;y=\\alpha_j\\bigl(x-(n-k)\\bigr)+\\beta_j .\n   \\]\n\n   Translation does not alter the slope, so each \\(\\lambda_j\\) is sunny.  \n   The set \\(\\{\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_k\\}\\) covers exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the image of \\(T_k\\) under the translation \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x+n-k,y)\\).\n\n3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   The union of the vertical lines \\(\\nu_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\)) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n   The union of the translated sunny lines \\(\\lambda_j\\) contains all points whose first coordinate is at least \\(n-k+1\\).  \n   Since the two subsets partition \\(T_n\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are not.  \n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct because the vertical lines have undefined slope whereas the \\(\\lambda_j\\) have finite slopes different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(n\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions, with precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness  \n\nSince we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe construction above shows that **every** integer in this set is attainable. Consequently the complete answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
