n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-\\!1=\\alpha\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)\n   \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{i.e. }y=\\alpha x+1-\\alpha n\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   The line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and, because its slope is sunny, it is a sunny line.  \n   Thus we have exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=3\\).**  \n   *Step 1.*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   *Step 2.*  After removing these columns the uncovered points form the set\n   \\[\n   T_3'=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-2\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a translate of \\(T_3\\).  \n   *Step 3.*  The six points of \\(T_3\\) can be covered by three distinct sunny lines, for example  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y =  \\;x      &&\\text{covers }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 &&\\text{covers }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + 5 &&\\text{covers }(1,3),(2,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes (\\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) are sunny.  \n   *Step 4.*  Translate each of the three lines by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\); the slopes are unchanged, so the translated lines remain sunny and together cover exactly the set \\(T_3'\\).  \n\n   Consequently we have \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny vertical lines and three sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=3\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines and that \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\) contains \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Let the two sunny lines be \\(S_1,S_2\\).  Remove them from the family; the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny, hence each of them is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   *Claim.*  Any non‑sunny line can contain at most one point of the set\n   \\[\n   R:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Indeed, the points of \\(R\\) have first coordinate either \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \n   Two distinct points of \\(R\\) with the same first coordinate lie on a vertical line, which is non‑sunny; but a vertical line can contain **both** points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\), etc.  However, the sum condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces that for \\(a=n\\) we have only the single point \\((n,1)\\); for \\(a=n-1\\) we have the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n   Consequently a vertical line can cover at most the two points with \\(a=n-1\\); a horizontal line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\) (because the second coordinate of points in \\(R\\) is at most \\(2\\)); a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(R\\) (the points of \\(R\\) have distinct values of \\(a+b\\)).  Hence any non‑sunny line covers **at most two** points of \\(R\\).\n\n   The set \\(R\\) contains exactly three points:\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2).\n   \\]\n\n   To cover these three points with the available lines we have two possibilities:\n\n   *Either* one of the two sunny lines contains two of them and the remaining point is covered by a non‑sunny line;  \n   *or* each sunny line contains exactly one of them and the third point must be covered by a non‑sunny line.\n\n   In the first sub‑case the non‑sunny line that covers the remaining point would have to be vertical (the only non‑sunny line that can pass through \\((n,1)\\) or \\((n-1,2)\\)).  A vertical line through \\((n,1)\\) is \\(x=n\\); this line also contains \\((n,1)\\) only, because \\((n,2)\\) does not belong to \\(T_n\\).  Similarly, a vertical line through \\((n-1,2)\\) is \\(x=n-1\\) and already contains \\((n-1,1)\\).  Hence the vertical line would duplicate a point already covered, contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be **distinct**.\n\n   In the second sub‑case we would need a non‑sunny line that passes through the single point left uncovered by the two sunny lines.  As argued above, any non‑sunny line that passes through one of the three points of \\(R\\) 