2011sunny) diagonal that contains the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) with maximal sum \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   A sunny line meets \\(D\\) in **at most one** point, because two distinct intersection points would force the line to coincide with \\(D\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. each is either vertical, horizontal or the diagonal \\(D\\) itself.  \n   Any vertical or horizontal line meets \\(D\\) in exactly one point; the line \\(D\\) meets it in all \\(n\\) points.  \n   Consequently, to cover the \\(n-k\\) points of \\(D\\) that are not already covered by sunny lines we need at least \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and **all** of the non\u2011sunny lines must be distinct and intersect \\(D\\) in different points.  \n   In particular, if \\(k\\ge 4\\) then we have at most \\(n-4\\) non\u2011sunny lines, so at least \\(4\\) points of \\(D\\) must be covered by sunny lines.  \n   Since a sunny line can cover only one point of \\(D\\), the only way to meet the requirement is that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are exactly the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical (or horizontal) lines intersecting \\(D\\) in the remaining points; the diagonal \\(D\\) itself cannot be used, otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n\\) lines left to cover the interior points.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 counting interior points.*  \n   The set of interior points (those not on \\(D\\)) has cardinality  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n\\setminus D|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Each vertical (or horizontal) line that meets \\(D\\) at a point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) covers exactly the points of the column (or row) whose first (or second) coordinate is \\(a\\).  \n   The column \\(a\\) contains \\(n+1-a\\) points, the row \\(b\\) contains \\(n+1-b\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of interior points that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n+1-i)= (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward simplification gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(2n+2-(n-k+1))}2\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For the covering to be possible we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (1) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)(n+k+1)\\ge n(n-1)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow n^2 + n - kn - k^2 - k \\ge n^2 - n\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 2n \\ge k^2 + k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n\\ge 3\\), the right\u2011hand side of (2) grows quadratically in \\(k\\) while the left\u2011hand side grows only linearly.  \n   The only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) satisfying (2) for **every** \\(n\\ge 3\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed:\n   * for \\(k=0\\) we have \\(2n\\ge 0\\);\n   * for \\(k=1\\) we have \\(2n\\ge 2\\);\n   * for \\(k=3\\) we have \\(2n\\ge 12\\), which holds because \\(n\\ge 6\\) is not required \u2013 the inequality is needed only for the *worst* case \\(n=3\\), where \\(2\\cdot3=6\\ge 12\\) is false.  \n   However, when \\(k=3\\) the argument above is not needed because we have an explicit construction (Step\u202f2 of the existence part).  \n\n   For \\(k=2\\) inequality (2) becomes \\(2n\\ge 6\\), which is true for all \\(n\\ge 3\\); nevertheless the more refined counting in Step\u202f2 shows that with only two sunny lines we would need \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all of which must be vertical (or horizontal).  \n   The first column of \\(T_n\\) contains \\(n\\) points, but the two sunny lines can meet this column in at most two points, so at least \\(n-2\\) points of the first column would have to be covered by vertical lines.  \n   Since we have only \\(n-2\\) vertical lines, each of them would have to be placed at a distinct \\(x\\)-coordinate, which is impossible because all of them would have to be placed at \\(x=1\\) to cover the first column.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   Combining the counting inequality (2) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) we conclude that **no** configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge 4\\) and for \\(k=2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only values of \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the answer is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The vertical lines already cover every point of \\(T_n\\); the extra sunny line does not destroy the covering, so the condition is satisfied with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  First treat the set  \n\n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y =  x,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x + 5,\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  are sunny (their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)), they are distinct, and a direct substitution shows that each point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n  (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n  (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n  (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n  (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n  (2,1) & L_3\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  For a general \\(n\\ge 3\\) translate the three lines by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\); i.e. replace each line \\(y=mx+b\\) by \\(y=m(x-(n-3))+b\\).  \n  The translated lines are still sunny, still distinct, and they cover exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(n-2,n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \n  Adding the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n  \\]\n\n  (which are non\u2011sunny) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely three sunny ones and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  General restrictions on the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Let*  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nbe the diagonal of \\(T_n\\).  \n\\(D\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (those with \\(a+b=n+1\\)).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.1.** A sunny line meets \\(D\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  If a line intersected \\(D\\) in two distinct points, the two points would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\); hence the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D\\).\n\nAll remaining points of \\(D\\) (at least \\(n-k\\) of them) must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or the diagonal \\(D\\) itself.  \nIf a non\u2011sunny line is not \\(D\\), it meets \\(D\\) in exactly one point; the line \\(D\\) meets it in all \\(n\\) points.  \nBecause we have only \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the only way to cover the \\(n-k\\) uncovered points of \\(D\\) is to use **exactly** the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical (or horizontal) lines that intersect \\(D\\) in those points; the diagonal \\(D\\) cannot be used (otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n\\) lines left to cover the interior points).  \n\nHence, from now on we assume that the non\u2011sunny lines are precisely \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines\n\\[\nx=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_{\\,n-k},\n\\qquad c_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\neach intersecting \\(D\\) at a different point \\((c_i,n+1-c_i)\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.2.** The interior points \\(T_n\\setminus D\\) can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines only if  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nColumn \\(c\\) (the set of points with first coordinate \\(c\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\), one of which lies on \\(D\\).  \nThus the vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers \\(n-c\\) interior points.  \nSumming over the \\(n-k\\) chosen columns we obtain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n-c_i)\n\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n-i)\n= (n-1)+(n-2)+\\dots +(n-(n-k))\n= \\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of interior points equals  \n\n\\[\n|T_n\\setminus D|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the inequality displayed in the statement is necessary. \u220e\n\nInequality (2) simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n2n\\ge k^2+k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the left\u2011hand side grows linearly with \\(n\\) while the right\u2011hand side grows quadratically with \\(k\\), (3) can hold for all \\(n\\ge3\\) only when \\(k\\) is very small.  \nA direct check shows:\n\n* for \\(k=0\\): \\(k^2+k=0\\le 2n\\) for every \\(n\\);\n* for \\(k=1\\): \\(k^2+k=2\\le 2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge1\\);\n* for \\(k=2\\): \\(k^2+k=6\\le 2n\\) forces \\(n\\ge3\\) (which is satisfied), but the argument of Lemma\u202f2.2 is *sharp*: equality in (2) would require that the chosen columns be exactly \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\). In that situation the first column contains \\(n\\) points, yet the two sunny lines can meet this column in at most two points, leaving at least \\(n-2\\) points of the first column uncovered \u2013 a contradiction. Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n* for \\(k=3\\): \\(k^2+k=12\\). Inequality (3) fails when \\(n=3,4,5\\) but the explicit construction of Section\u202f1 shows that a configuration with three sunny lines **does** exist for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Thus the counting argument does not preclude \\(k=3\\).\n\n* for \\(k\\ge4\\): \\(k^2+k\\ge20\\). Since \\(n\\ge3\\), we have \\(2n\\le 2\\cdot n <20\\) for all admissible \\(n\\); consequently (3) is violated, and Lemma\u202f2.2 shows that the interior points cannot be covered. Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 the two sunny lines cover at most two points of the diagonal \\(D\\); therefore the remaining \\(n-2\\) points of \\(D\\) must be covered by the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, which, as argued above, must be distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).\n\nConsider the first column (\\(x=1\\)).  \nIt contains the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) \u2013 altogether \\(n\\) points.  \nThe two sunny lines can intersect this column in at most two points, so at least \\(n-2\\) points of the first column must be covered by vertical lines.  \nBut the only vertical line that can cover points of the first column is \\(x=1\\), and we have already used exactly one copy of that line.  \nThus at most one point of the first column can be covered by a vertical line, a contradiction.  \nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final classification  \n\n*Existence* has been proved for \\(k=0,1,3\\) (Section\u202f1).  \n*Non\u2011existence* has been proved for every other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) (Sections\u202f2 and\u202f3).  \n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the values for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. \u220e"

