ing a vertical line in at most one point.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Order the vertical lines so that \\(c_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) (replacing a larger \\(c_i\\) by a missing smaller integer never reduces coverage).  \n   Consider the first column not covered by a vertical line, namely column \\(x=n-k+1\\).  \n   This column contains exactly \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k+1,1),\\;(n-k+1,2),\\dots ,(n-k+1,k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the vertical lines do not meet these points, they must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   A sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point, therefore the points in (2) lie on **different** sunny lines; consequently each sunny line is forced to pass through a distinct point of (2).\n\n   Move one column to the right, to \\(x=n-k+2\\).  \n   Column \\(x=n-k+2\\) contains only \\(k-1\\) integer points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Extending the \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines from column \\(n-k+1\\) to column \\(n-k+2\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates (otherwise two lines would coincide).  \n   Because there are only \\(k-1\\) integer points available in that column, at least one of the sunny lines lands on a non‑integer point and therefore does **not** cover any new point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot together cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns, a contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible.\n\n5. **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n   With the same reduction we have vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and two sunny lines that must pass through the two points of column \\(x=n-1\\), namely \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n   Extending these two lines to column \\(x=n\\) gives two distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, but column \\(x=n\\) contains only the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Consequently at most one of the two sunny lines meets an integer point in column \\(n\\); the other line fails to cover any new point, leaving \\((n,1)\\) uncovered.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of step 2 show that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable.  \n   Steps 4 and 5 prove that no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   Hence the complete set of admissible values is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); equivalently the line is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  together with the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-2n+1 .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n  Substituting \\(x=n\\) in (3) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \n  Every other point of \\(T_n\\) has first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) and therefore belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  For \\(n=3\\) the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  cover the six points of \\(T_3\\).  \n  Translate each line of (4) by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\); i.e. replace \\(x\\) by \\(x-(n-3)\\).  \n  The three resulting lines are\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+3}{2},\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x + 2n+1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus all three are sunny.  \n\n  **Verification for the last three columns.**  \n  The columns \\(x=n-2,n-1,n\\) contain respectively  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  x=n-2 & (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2)\\\\[2pt]\n  x=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n-1,3)\\\\[2pt]\n  x=n   & (n,1),\\;(n,2),\\;(n,3),\\;(n,4)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  (points whose sum exceeds \\(n+1\\) are omitted).  \n  Substituting each of these points into the equations of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) shows that every point belongs to at least one of the three lines; a short table is given below.\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n  \\text{point} & L_1 & L_2 & L_3 \\\\ \\hline\n  (n-2,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-2,2) & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-1,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{yes}\\\\\n  (n-1,2) & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-1,3) & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n,1)   & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n,2)   & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n,3)   & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{yes}\\\\\n  (n,4)   & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{no (outside }T_n\\text{)}\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  (All points listed indeed satisfy the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).)  \n  Thus the three lines (5) cover every point whose first coordinate is \\(n-2,n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \n\n  Adding the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n  \\]\n\n  (which are non‑sunny) covers the remaining columns.  \n  Consequently we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones that covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nReflecting the whole picture across the line \\(y=x\\) maps \\(T_n\\) onto itself and turns every horizontal non‑sunny line into a vertical one while leaving sunny lines sunny.  \nHence, without loss of generality, we may assume that **all** non‑sunny lines are vertical.  \n\nThus a configuration satisfying the problem consists of  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_{\\,n-k}\\) with \\(1\\le c_i\\le n\\);  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each intersecting a vertical line in at most one point.\n\nIf some integer \\(m\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is missing from the set \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_{\\,n-k}\\}\\) while a larger integer \\(M\\) is present, we may replace the line \\(x=M\\) by \\(x=m\\); this can only increase the set of covered points. Consequently we may (and shall) assume  \n\n\\[\nc_i=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nConsider the first column **not** covered by a vertical line, namely column  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the vertical lines stop at \\(x=n-k\\), the points of this column that belong to \\(T_n\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-k+1,1),\\;(n-k+1,2),\\dots ,(n-k+1,k).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNo vertical line meets any of the points in (7); therefore each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a distinct point of (7).  \nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines are forced to pass through the \\(k\\) points listed in (7).\n\nNow look at the next column, \\(x=n-k+2\\).  \nIt contains only \\(k-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n(n-k+2,1),\\;(n-k+2,2),\\dots ,(n-k+2,k-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line already passes through a point of column \\(n-k+1\\); extending the line to column \\(n-k+2\\) yields a unique \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nBecause the lines are distinct, the \\(k\\) obtained \\(y\\)-coordinates are also distinct; otherwise two lines would coincide.  \nThus the set of intersection points of the sunny lines with column \\(n-k+2\\) consists of **\\(k\\) distinct points**.  \n\nSince column \\(n-k+2\\) contains only the \\(k-1\\) integer points listed in (8), at least one of the sunny lines meets column \\(n-k+2\\) at a non‑integer point and therefore does **not** cover any new point of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot together cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume \\(k=2\\).  \nBy the reduction of Section 2 we have the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand two sunny lines.  \nColumn \\(x=n-1\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\n(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich are not covered by (9); therefore the two sunny lines must pass through the two points of (10), one point per line.\n\nProceed to column \\(x=n\\).  \nIt contains only the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \nExtending the two distinct sunny lines from column \\(n-1\\) to column \\(n\\) yields two distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, but only one of them can be the integer \\(1\\).  \nThus at most one of the two sunny lines meets an integer point of column \\(n\\); the other line meets a non‑integer point and contributes nothing further.  \nConsequently the point \\((n,1)\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  \n\nTherefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the classification  \n\nThe explicit families exhibited in Section 1 show that the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=3\n\\]\n\nare attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSections 3 and 4 prove that no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎"
