on\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nReflecting the whole picture across the line \\(y=x\\) maps \\(T_n\\) onto itself and turns every horizontal non\u2011sunny line into a vertical one while leaving sunny lines sunny.  \nHence, without loss of generality, we may assume that **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.  \n\nThus a configuration satisfying the problem consists of  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_{\\,n-k}\\) with \\(1\\le c_i\\le n\\);  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each intersecting a vertical line in at most one point.\n\nIf some integer \\(m\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is missing from the set \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_{\\,n-k}\\}\\) while a larger integer \\(M\\) is present, we may replace the line \\(x=M\\) by \\(x=m\\); this can only increase the set of covered points. Consequently we may (and shall) assume  \n\n\\[\nc_i=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nConsider the first column **not** covered by a vertical line, namely column  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the vertical lines stop at \\(x=n-k\\), the points of this column that belong to \\(T_n\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-k+1,1),\\;(n-k+1,2),\\dots ,(n-k+1,k).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNo vertical line meets any of the points in (7); therefore each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a distinct point of (7).  \nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines are forced to pass through the \\(k\\) points listed in (7).\n\nNow look at the next column, \\(x=n-k+2\\).  \nIt contains only \\(k-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n(n-k+2,1),\\;(n-k+2,2),\\dots ,(n-k+2,k-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line already passes through a point of column \\(n-k+1\\); extending the line to column \\(n-k+2\\) yields a unique \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nBecause the lines are distinct, the \\(k\\) obtained \\(y\\)-coordinates are also distinct; otherwise two lines would coincide.  \nThus the set of intersection points of the sunny lines with column \\(n-k+2\\) consists of **\\(k\\) distinct points**.  \n\nSince column \\(n-k+2\\) contains only the \\(k-1\\) integer points listed in (8), at least one of the sunny lines meets column \\(n-k+2\\) at a non\u2011integer point and therefore does **not** cover any new point of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot together cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume \\(k=2\\).  \nBy the reduction of Section\u202f2 we have the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand two sunny lines.  \nColumn \\(x=n-1\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\n(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich are not covered by (9); therefore the two sunny lines must pass through the two points of (10), one point per line.\n\nProceed to column \\(x=n\\).  \nIt contains only the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \nExtending the two distinct sunny lines from column \\(n-1\\) to column \\(n\\) yields two distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, but only one of them can be the integer \\(1\\).  \nThus at most one of the two sunny lines meets an integer point of column \\(n\\); the other line meets a non\u2011integer point and contributes nothing further.  \nConsequently the point \\((n,1)\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  \n\nTherefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the classification  \n\nThe explicit families exhibited in Section\u202f1 show that the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=3\n\\]\n\nare attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSections\u202f3 and\u202f4