the last \\(k\\) columns \\(x=n-k+1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   In column \\(x=n-k+r\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le k\\)) the admissible integer ordinates are \\(1,2,\\dots ,k-r+1\\).  \n   *Lemma.* A sunny line can contain integer points in at most **two** of these \\(k\\) columns.  \n   *Proof.* If a sunny line contained integer points in three successive columns, the three points would have ordinates forming an arithmetic progression with common difference equal to the line’s slope. Because the maximal ordinate drops by exactly one when we move one column to the right, the only possible common difference is \\(-1\\), which would give slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\n   Hence the total number of points of the last \\(k\\) columns that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   The last \\(k\\) columns contain  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points. For \\(k\\ge4\\) we have \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2>2k\\) (equivalently \\(k(k-3)>0\\)).  \n   Therefore the sunny lines cannot cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines lie in the first \\(n-k\\) columns and therefore meet none of the points in the last \\(k\\) columns.  \n   Consequently some point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered – a contradiction.  \n   Hence no configuration exists for \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The last two columns are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   x=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\n   x=n   & (n,1)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   The two vertical non‑sunny lines occupy columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-2\\) and cannot meet any of these three points.  \n   Because a vertical line cannot pass through two points having the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) must be covered by the two sunny lines, one point per line.  \n\n   Suppose a sunny line passes through \\((n-1,1)\\). Its equation is  \n   \\[\n   y=m\\bigl(x-(n-1)\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\]\n   At \\(x=n\\) we obtain \\(y=m+1\\). For this to equal the integer ordinate \\(1\\) of the point \\((n,1)\\) we would need \\(m=0\\), i.e. a horizontal line, which is not sunny.  \n   The same argument applies to a sunny line through \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n   Hence neither sunny line can also pass through \\((n,1)\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) remains uncovered – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of step 2 show that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Steps 5 and 6 prove that no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   Hence the complete set of admissible values is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  None of them is sunny, and a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  together with the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-2n+1 .\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n  Substituting \\(x=n\\) in (1) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  \n  Every other point of \\(T_n\\) has first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) and therefore belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Place the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  The remaining six points of \\(T_n\\) lie in the last three columns:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  x=n-2 & (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3)\\\\\n  x=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\n  x=n   & (n,1)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  The three sunny lines\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x+2n-1\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n  Substituting the six points of (3) into the equations of (4) gives the following table:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n  \\text{point} & L_1 & L_2 & L_3 \\\\ \\hline\n  (n-2,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-2,2) & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-2,3) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{yes}\\\\\n  (n-1,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{yes}\\\\\n  (n-1,2) & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n,1)   & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  (Each “yes’’ means that the point satisfies the corresponding linear equation; a short computation verifies each entry.)  \n  Thus every point of (3) lies on at least one of the three lines in (4).  \n  Together with the vertical lines (2) all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a favourable configuration\n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*If a non‑sunny line is not vertical*, it meets any fixed column \\(x=c\\) in at most one point of \\(T_n\\) (because the column consists of points with distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates).  \nA vertical line, on the other hand, can meet **all** points of a column.  \n\nHence, for the purpose of proving that a certain value of \\(k\\) is impossible, we may replace every non‑vertical non‑sunny line by a vertical line placed in a column that already contains a point of the original line. This replacement cannot decrease the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered, because the new vertical line covers at least the point that the old line covered in that column, and possibly additional points of the same column.  \n\nConsequently, if a configuration with given \\(k\\) exists, there also exists a configuration in which\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are **distinct vertical lines**, and  \n* these vertical lines occupy the first \\(n-k\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).\n\nFrom now on we work under this assumption; it is the *most favourable* situation for covering the points of the last \\(k\\) columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A bound on how many points a sunny line can cover in the last \\(k\\) columns\n\nConsider the last \\(k\\) columns  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nIn column \\(x=n-k+r\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le k\\)) the admissible integer ordinates are  \n\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,k-r+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.**  \nA sunny line can contain integer points in at most **two** of these \\(k\\) columns.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains integer points in three distinct columns, say  \n\n\\[\n(n-k+r_1,\\,y_1),\\;(n-k+r_2,\\,y_2),\\;(n-k+r_3,\\,y_3)\n\\]\n\nwith \\(r_1<r_2<r_3\\).  \nBecause the points are collinear, the slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{r_2-r_1}=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{r_3-r_2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(y_{i+1}\\le y_i-1\\) when we move one column to the right, so the only possible integer difference between successive \\(y\\)’s is \\(-1\\). Equation (6) therefore forces the common difference to be \\(-1\\), which yields slope \\(-1\\). This contradicts the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n---\n\nTherefore each sunny line contributes at most two integer points among the last \\(k\\) columns. With \\(k\\) sunny lines the total number of points of the last \\(k\\) columns that can be covered is at most \\(2k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nThe last \\(k\\) columns contain  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints (by (5)).  \nFor \\(k\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2>2k\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k(k-3)>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the sunny lines cannot cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns.  \nThe \\(n-k\\) vertical lines lie in the first \\(n-k\\) columns and therefore meet none of the points in the last \\(k\\) columns.  \nConsequently at least one point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered – a contradiction.  \nThus no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nWith \\(k=2\\) the last two columns are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nx=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\nx=n   & (n,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe two non‑sunny vertical lines occupy columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-2\\) and cannot meet any point of (7).  \n\nA vertical line cannot pass through both points of column \\(n-1\\); therefore the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) must be covered by the two sunny lines, one point per line.\n\nSuppose a sunny line passes through \\((n-1,1)\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=m\\bigl(x-(n-1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nAt \\(x=n\\) we obtain \\(y=m+1\\). For this to equal the ordinate \\(1\\) of the point \\((n,1)\\) we would need \\(m=0\\), i.e. a horizontal line, which is not sunny.  \nHence the line through \\((n-1,1)\\) cannot also pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe same argument applies to a sunny line through \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n\nThus neither sunny line can cover the point \\((n,1)\\); that point remains uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Completion of the classification\n\nThe constructions of Section 1 exhibit configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSections 4 and 5 prove that no configuration exists for any other non‑negative integer \\(k\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor all integers \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎"
