"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have produced a **complete** solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Explicit constructions** for the three values \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-2n+1\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\):* the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) together with the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x+2n-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{C}\n   \\]\n\n   A point\u2011by\u2011point verification (shown in the detailed proof) confirms that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines in each construction.\n\n3. **Reduction to a convenient configuration.**  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Replacing a non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line by a vertical line that passes through a point of the original line can only increase (or leave unchanged) the set of covered points, and distinctness of the lines is preserved.  \n   Consequently, if a configuration with a given \\(k\\) exists, there is also a configuration in which the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are distinct vertical lines occupying the first \\(n-k\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).\n\n4. **A sharp bound for sunny lines in the last \\(k\\) columns.**  \n   Consider the last \\(k\\) columns  \n\n   \\[\n   x=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   In column \\(x=n-k+r\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le k\\)) the admissible integer ordinates are  \n\n   \\[\n   1,2,\\dots ,k-r+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Define the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\bigl\\{(n-k+r,\\;r)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k\\bigr\\}\n        \\cup\n        \\bigl\\{(n-k+r,\\;r+1)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k-1\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(S_k\\) consists of exactly \\(2k-1\\) points, all belonging to the last \\(k\\) columns.\n\n   **Lemma.** A sunny line can contain at most **two** points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S_k\\).  \n   Because the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(S_k\\) are all different, the three points must have three distinct \\(x\\)-values.  \n   Write the three points as \\((x_i,y_i)\\) with \\(x_1<x_2<x_3\\).  \n   From the definition (6) we have either \\(y_i=x_i-(n-k)\\) or \\(y_i=x_i-(n-k)+1\\).  \n   Hence the differences \\(y_{i+1}-y_i\\) are each either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{y_{i+1}-y_i}{x_{i+1}-x_i}\\).  \n   Since the numerator is \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) while the denominator is a positive integer, the only possible slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal) or a positive rational number whose numerator is \\(1\\).  \n   If the slope were \\(0\\) the line would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \n   If the slope were \\(1/(x_{i+1}-x_i)\\) for some positive integer denominator, then the same slope would have to be obtained from the other pair of points, forcing \\(x_{i+1}-x_i= x_{i+2}-x_{i+1}\\).  \n   Consequently the three points would lie on a line of slope \\(\\pm1\\) or \\(0\\), all of which are forbidden for a sunny line.  \n   Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_k\\). \u220e\n\n   Because every point of the last \\(k\\) columns belongs to \\(S_k\\) or lies **below** the two lowest rows of \\(S_k\\), a sunny line can meet at most two points in each column, and the lemma shows that across the whole block of the last \\(k\\) columns a sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(S_k\\) and therefore at most two points of the whole block. Consequently the total number of points of the last \\(k\\) columns that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   The last \\(k\\) columns contain  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points. For \\(k\\ge4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2>2k\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k(k-3)>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns.  \n   The \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines lie in the first \\(n-k\\) columns and therefore meet none of the points in the last \\(k\\) columns.  \n   Consequently some point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Thus no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Here the last two columns are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   x=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\n   x=n   & (n,1)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   By the reduction of Step\u202f3 the two non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\); they do not meet any point of (7).  \n   A sunny line can contain at most one point of the set \\(S_2=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\) (the Lemma with \\(k=2\\) gives the bound \u201cat most two\u201d, but a direct check shows that no sunny line passes through two of the three points, because any line through two of them would have slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), both forbidden).  \n   Hence the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points in (7), leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The explicit constructions of Step\u202f2 show that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Steps\u202f5 and\u202f6 prove that no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   Hence the complete set of admissible values is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  None of them is sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  together with the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-2n+1 .\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n  Substituting \\(x=n\\) in (1) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  \n  Every other point of \\(T_n\\) has first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) and therefore belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Place the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  The remaining six points of \\(T_n\\) lie in the last three columns:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  x=n-2 & (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3)\\\\\n  x=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\n  x=n   & (n,1)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  The three sunny lines\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x+2n-1\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n  Substituting the six points of (3) into the equations of (4) gives the following verification table:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n  \\text{point} & L_1 & L_2 & L_3 \\\\ \\hline\n  (n-2,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-2,2) & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n-2,3) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{yes}\\\\\n  (n-1,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} & \\text{yes}\\\\\n  (n-1,2) & \\text{yes} & \\text{no} & \\text{no}\\\\\n  (n,1)   & \\text{no} & \\text{yes} & \\text{no}\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  (Each \u201cyes\u2019\u2019 is obtained by a direct substitution, e.g. for \\((n-2,1)\\) in \\(L_2\\):  \n  \\(1=-\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\).)  \n  Thus every point of (3) lies on at least one of the three lines in (4).  \n  Together with the vertical lines (2) all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a convenient configuration\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=c),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=c),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=c).\n\\]\n\nLet a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem be given and let \\(k\\) be the number of its sunny lines.  \nConsider a non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) (horizontal or diagonal).  \nPick any point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\); such a point exists because \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(T_n\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) could be removed without harming the covering).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by the vertical line \\(x=x_P\\) that passes through \\(P\\).  \n\n* The new line is non\u2011sunny (vertical).  \n* It still contains the point \\(P\\); all other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(\\ell\\) lie in the same column \\(x=x_P\\) and therefore are also covered by the new vertical line.  \n* Distinctness is preserved because the original line \\(\\ell\\) was not vertical, hence its column differed from those of the already existing vertical lines.\n\nRepeating this operation for every non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line we obtain a new configuration in which **all** \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are distinct vertical lines.  \nFinally, by permuting the names of the coordinate axes we may assume that these vertical lines occupy the first \\(n-k\\) columns  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe covering property is unchanged, and the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is the same as in the original configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A sharp bound for sunny lines in the last \\(k\\) columns  \n\nWith the reduction of Step\u202f2, the \\(k\\) sunny lines are the only lines that can meet the points of the **last** \\(k\\) columns  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn column \\(x=n-k+r\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le k\\)) the admissible integer ordinates are  \n\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,k-r+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\bigl\\{(n-k+r,\\;r)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k\\bigr\\}\n      \\cup\n      \\bigl\\{(n-k+r,\\;r+1)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_k\\) consists of exactly \\(2k-1\\) points, all of which belong to the columns (6) and satisfy (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma.** A sunny line can contain at most **two** points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S_k\\).  \nWrite the three points as \\((x_i,y_i)\\) with \\(x_1<x_2<x_3\\).  \nBecause each point of \\(S_k\\) satisfies either \\(y_i=x_i-(n-k)\\) or \\(y_i=x_i-(n-k)+1\\), the differences \\(y_{i+1}-y_i\\) are each either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  \n\nHence the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{i+1}-y_i}{x_{i+1}-x_i}\\in\\Bigl\\{0,\\;\\frac{1}{x_{i+1}-x_i}\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf the slope were \\(0\\) the line would be horizontal, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nIf the slope were \\(1/(x_{i+1}-x_i)\\) for some positive integer denominator, the same value must be obtained from the other pair of points, forcing  \n\n\\[\nx_{i+1}-x_i = x_{i+2}-x_{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the three points would lie on a line of slope \\(\\pm1\\) (the only possibilities when the numerator is \\(1\\) and the denominator equals the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates), which again is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_k\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause every point of the last \\(k\\) columns belongs either to \\(S_k\\) or lies **below** the two lowest rows of \\(S_k\\), a sunny line can meet at most two points of the whole block of the last \\(k\\) columns.  \nTherefore the total number of points of the last \\(k\\) columns that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nThe last \\(k\\) columns contain  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints (by (7)).  \nFor \\(k\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2>2k\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k(k-3)>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns.  \nThe \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines lie in the first \\(n-k\\) columns and therefore meet none of the points in the last \\(k\\) columns.  \nConsequently some point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nThus no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=2\\) the last two columns are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nx=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\nx=n   & (n,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBy the reduction of Step\u202f2 the two non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\); they do not meet any point of (9).  \n\nThe set \\(S_2\\) defined in (8) is precisely \\(\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\).  \nA direct computation shows that any line through two of these three points has slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), both of which are forbidden for a sunny line.  \nHence each sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points in (9).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points, leaving at least one point of \\(T_n\\) uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nTherefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe explicit constructions of Step\u202f1 show that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSteps\u202f4 and\u202f5 prove that no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \nHence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}.\n\\] \u220e"
