{array}\n  \\]\n\n  *Example of a substitution:* for \\((n-2,1)\\) in \\(L_2\\),\n\n  \\[\n  1 = -\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\n  \\]\n\n  which holds after simplifying the right\u2013hand side to \\(1\\).  \n  The other entries are checked analogously; each point of (3) lies on at least one of the lines in (4).  \n\n  Together with the vertical lines (2) all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a configuration with distinct vertical non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions: horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nWe claim that we can replace the non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny lines by vertical ones while preserving distinctness and the covering property.\n\n*For a non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\):*  \n\\(\\ell\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at least one point; choose any such point \\(P\\).  \nLet \\(c\\) be the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nThe new line still contains \\(P\\) and therefore still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by \\(\\ell\\) (they all lie in the same column \\(x=c\\)).  \n\nNow we must guarantee that the vertical lines obtained from different original non\u2011sunny lines are **distinct**.  \nThere are \\(n\\) columns \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and only \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nWe assign to each non\u2011sunny line a column as follows.\n\nOrder the non\u2011sunny lines arbitrarily as \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-k}\\).  \nFor \\(\\ell_1\\) pick any column that meets \\(\\ell_1\\) in \\(T_n\\); call it \\(c_1\\).  \nHaving chosen distinct columns \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_{j-1}\\) for the first \\(j-1\\) lines, consider \\(\\ell_j\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_j\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at least one column, and at most \\(j-1\\) columns have already been used, there remains a column \\(c_j\\) not yet used that contains a point of \\(\\ell_j\\cap T_n\\).  \nAssign \\(c_j\\) to \\(\\ell_j\\) and replace \\(\\ell_j\\) by the vertical line \\(x=c_j\\).\n\nBecause at each step fewer than \\(n\\) columns have been occupied, the greedy procedure never gets stuck; thus we obtain \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines.  \nThe sunny lines are left unchanged, so the total number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).  \n\nHence **without loss of generality** we may assume that the non\u2011sunny lines are the distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{R}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A bound for sunny lines in the last \\(k\\) columns  \n\nWith the reduction (R), the only lines that can meet points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(n-k\\) are the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConsider the last \\(k\\) columns  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn column \\(x=n-k+r\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le k\\)) the admissible ordinates are  \n\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,k-r+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\bigl\\{(n-k+r,\\;r)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k\\bigr\\}\n        \\cup\n        \\bigl\\{(n-k+r,\\;r+1)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(S_k\\) consists of exactly \\(2k-1\\) points, all belonging to the block (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma.** A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S_k\\).  \nWrite them as \\((x_i,y_i)\\) with \\(x_1<x_2<x_3\\).  \nEach point satisfies either \\(y_i=x_i-(n-k)\\) or \\(y_i=x_i-(n-k)+1\\); consequently each difference \\(y_{i+1}-y_i\\) equals \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  \nHence the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{i+1}-y_i}{x_{i+1}-x_i}\\in\\Bigl\\{0,\\;\\frac{1}{x_{i+1}-x_i}\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf the slope were \\(0\\) the line would be horizontal, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nIf the slope were \\(\\frac{1}{d}\\) with integer \\(d>0\\), the same value must be obtained from the other pair of points, forcing \\(x_{i+1}-x_i=x_{i+2}-x_{i+1}\\).  \nThus the three points would lie on a line of slope \\(\\pm1\\) or \\(0\\), all of which are prohibited for a sunny line.  \nTherefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_k\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause every point of the last \\(k\\) columns belongs either to \\(S_k\\) or lies **below** the two lowest rows of \\(S_k\\), a sunny line can meet at most two points of the whole block (5).  \nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of the last \\(k\\) columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nThe last \\(k\\) columns contain  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints (by (7)).  \nFor \\(k\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2>2k\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k(k-3)>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of the last \\(k\\) columns.  \nThe \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (R) lie in the first \\(n-k\\) columns and meet none of those points, so some point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nHence no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=2\\) the last two columns are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nx=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\\nx=n   & (n,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBy the reduction (R) the two non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\); they do not meet any point of (8).  \n\nThe set \\(S_2\\) defined in (6) is exactly \\(\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\).  \nA direct computation shows that any line through two of these three points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), both forbidden for a sunny line.  \nTherefore each sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points, so the two sunny lines can cover at most two of them, leaving at least one point of \\(T_n\\) uncovered.  \nThus \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe constructions of Step\u202f1 demonstrate that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSteps\u202f4 and\u202f5 prove that no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \nHence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}.\n\\] \u220e"
