ad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nNone of them is sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n-----\n\n##### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)  \n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x-2n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThe only points not yet covered have first coordinate \\(a=n\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\).  Substituting \\(x=n\\) into (1) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n-----\n\n##### 1.3  \\(k=3\\)  \n\nTake the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining six points of \\(T_n\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nx=n-2 & (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3)\\\\[2pt]\nx=n-1 & (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\\\[2pt]\nx=n   & (n,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x-n+3,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x+2n-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so all three are sunny.\n\n*Verification that the six points of (3) are covered.*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{point} & L_1 & L_2 & L_3 \\\\ \\hline\n(n-2,1) & \\text{no} &\n\\displaystyle 1 = -\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1 & \\text{no} \\\\[6pt]\n(n-2,2) & \\displaystyle 2 = (n-2)-n+3 & \\text{no} & \\text{no} \\\\[6pt]\n(n-2,3) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} &\n\\displaystyle 3 = -2 (n-2)+2n-1 \\\\[6pt]\n(n-1,1) & \\text{no} & \\text{no} &\n\\displaystyle 1 = -2 (n-1)+2n-1 \\\\[6pt]\n(n-1,2) & \\displaystyle 2 = (n-1)-n+3 & \\text{no} & \\text{no} \\\\[6pt]\n(n,1)   & \\text{no}