red number of points exceeds the maximal possible coverage is **invalid** for \\(k\\ge5\\). The impossibility proof therefore collapses for all \\(k\\ge5\\).  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the argument depends on the erroneous bound from the previous step.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)  \n\n**Quote:**  \n*\u201cWhen \\(k=2\\) the uncovered points are \\(P=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\). \u2026 a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\). Consequently at least three sunny lines are needed, contradicting \\(k=2\\). Thus \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\u201d*  \n\n**Verification:**  \nThe description of the uncovered set after using \\(n-2\\) vertical lines is correct. Any line through two of the three points has slope \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or is vertical, all of which are forbidden for sunny lines. Hence a sunny line can indeed contain at most one of the three points, forcing at least three sunny lines. The reasoning is sound.  \n\n**Classification:** No issue \u2013 this part of the solution is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Existence for the remaining values  \n\n**Quote:**  \n*\u201cThe explicit families of lines exhibited in Step\u202f1 satisfy all requirements for \\(k=0,1,3\\). \u2026\u201d*  \n\n**Verification:**  \nAs verified in Section\u202f1, the constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) are correct.  \n\n**Classification:** No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Final answer  \n\n**Quote:**  \n*\u201c\\(\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\u201d*  \n\n**Verification:**  \nThe answer coincides with the constructions provided, but the **proof** that no other \\(k\\) are possible relies on the critical errors identified in Sections\u202f2\u20136. Therefore the conclusion is **not justified** by the presented argument.  \n\n**Classification:** The answer may be correct, but the solution\u2019s justification is invalid.\n\n--- \n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution correctly handles the constructive cases \\(k=0,1,3\\) an